{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Abraham was old, advanced in days.</b> Many commentators wonder: was it not already stated, ״וְאַבְרָהָם וְשָׂרָה זְקֵנִים בָּאִים בַּיָּמִים״ (Genesis 18:11)? And there were some thirty-seven years between these two times. And in Bereshit Rabbah (48:16) it concludes that the Holy One, blessed be He, restored them both to the days of their youth, and this answer suffices, except that it has no support from Scripture. And there are those who say that ״בָּאִים״ (coming) indicates that specific time, for it should have said ״בָּאוּ״ (they came). Rather, ״בָּאִים״ means that then they were coming and going in [i.e., progressing through] the days, but here it says ״בָּא״, meaning that he had already come into the days — and this is a precise reading." "And according to its plain sense we may say that two are better than one, and a threefold cord is not quickly broken (Ecclesiastes 4:12). For a good wife adds life to her husband, as it says (Ecclesiastes 9:9), ״רְאֵה חַיִּים עִם אִשָּׁה״ — 'enjoy life with a wife' — and a good son also adds life to his father, for whoever raises a righteous son is as though he did not die — all the more so during his lifetime. Therefore, from the birth of Isaac until the death of Sarah the cord was threefold, for vitality was added to Abraham both from the side of his wife and from the side of his son Isaac. And all those days he stood at one station; no weakness was added to him as is the way of the old, for they were days of joy and blessing. But before the birth of Isaac he had joy only from his wife, and after Sarah's death he had joy only from his son. Therefore those two periods were equal: just as it was near the time of Isaac's birth, so it became after Sarah's death. Therefore one expression was used for both these times, even though there was a great distance between them. For this reason he hastened immediately to marry him [Isaac] to a wife, for he thought that perhaps his [new] wife would fill Sarah's place and he would rejoice in her just as in his wife indeed. And so it was: before the sun of Sarah had set, the sun of Rebecca rose, and she became exactly like Sarah, as Rashi explained nearby (Genesis 24:67), ״וַיְבִאֶהָ הָאֹהֱלָה שָׂרָה אִמּוֹ״ — 'and he brought her into the tent, Sarah his mother' — and behold, she is his mother, etc. And this is a correct connection in the juxtaposition of this verse to the marriage of Isaac." "And as for what it writes, ״בָּא בַּיָּמִים״ — 'advanced in days' — for apparently it should have said ״יָצָא מִן הַיָּמִים״ — 'gone out of the days.' Rather, it is because the measure among the wicked is that the days of youth are for them days of light and bright day, for they are good to them for physical pleasures, for that is the entirety of their purpose. And the days of old age are for them night and not day, for the world grows dark on their account, since they no longer taste of the honey at the end of the staff. And therefore the former days are better to them than these, and it is as though they have gone out from days into nights. But among the righteous the order is reversed, for they come from the nights into the days, since the days of old age are better to them for the acquisition of wisdom. And along the lines of what is said (Ecclesiastes 7:10), ״אַל תֹּאמַר מֶה הָיָה שֶׁהַיָּמִים הָרִאשֹׁנִים הָיוּ טוֹבִים מֵאֵלֶּה, כִּי לֹא מֵחָכְמָה שָׁאַלְתָּ עַל זֶה״ — 'Do not say: what was, that the former days were better than these, for not out of wisdom did you ask concerning this' — for one who says so does not care about wisdom. Therefore, concerning the righteous it says ״בָּא בַּיָּמִים״ — he has come into the days — in their old age." "And there are those who say that therefore it mentions ״בָּא בַּיָּמִים״ to indicate that old age did not seize him on account of suffering, but rather because he had come into the days — for HaShem had blessed him in everything and he had no suffering at all. And we may say that this is why he called Eliezer ״זְקַן בֵּיתוֹ״ — 'the elder of his house' — for he was old by virtue of the fact that the needs of the household were laid upon him. And Scripture informs us that because Abraham was old, he did not have the strength to go alone, and he did not want to send Isaac either, for he said, ״פֶּן אָמוּת פִּתְאוֹם, וּמִי יָשִׂים יָדוֹ עַל עֵינָי״ — 'lest I die suddenly, and who will lay his hand upon my eyes.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "In the phrase 'to marry him to a wife,' the masculine suffix presumably refers to Isaac, though Abraham is the subject of the preceding clause; the reference is resolved by context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:35.560601729Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}